#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "870077e3-99f9-52b9-ac39-26bbc3827466" :question/math-academy-id "e-9035" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln(x + y)$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x + y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x + y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= \\ln z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln z+1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(z) = \\ln z + 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "d3145755-b414-57e5-8039-04ceb1a3a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9037" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y$.\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x,y) = x+C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z+1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{z + 1}\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 1 \\\\\n∫\\frac{1}{z + 1}dz &= ∫dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid z + 1 \\mid &= x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln{\\mid x+y+1 \\mid} = x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = \\ln{\\mid x+y+1 \\mid}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50ecc4f-1b95-5bad-a7ab-7236fffbc836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{z + 5} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163377} {:answer/id #uuid "da9cf03a-ffe5-5cef-b628-0ca848c3e460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{z} - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163378} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5d8406-deaf-5d76-aa5b-c05f83c10052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{z} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163379} {:answer/id #uuid "152e1265-ac4b-5f2d-9bbb-022edcfff398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - 5e^{z + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163380} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9c4bb9-e45a-5c15-be44-36b32a383fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - e^{z + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50ecc4f-1b95-5bad-a7ab-7236fffbc836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{z + 5} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163377} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7af5785-1318-5b3e-94a2-5a0249e13841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163376}] :question/id #uuid "d5d4c4f2-83ab-5c6a-b2e1-62527a7b057c" :question/math-academy-id "q-107797" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x + 2y + 5}$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z = x + 2y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eea73c09-363d-57f9-8c9f-2562eab21266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163383} {:answer/id #uuid "f57169c5-806c-514b-8136-abb7e1f93a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163384} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8edcf4-93dd-5d92-8a94-a8c08b36a2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163385} {:answer/id #uuid "2399e673-d222-5bf6-b2d3-9da89ae906b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163386} {:answer/id #uuid "5979943d-5849-5c26-928e-c3208db1cf4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8edcf4-93dd-5d92-8a94-a8c08b36a2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163385} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c1201b-ea79-5111-a44b-c9cf706e967e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163382}] :question/id #uuid "d3dd7c3d-2958-59a2-969e-34c4f6fbd85e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107814" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sqrt{y + 3x + 2} - 3$.\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x, y) = x + C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x, y)$?"}]}
